[Music]  do we need vaccine mandates  [Music]  these protesters in new york say it's  their right to decide what goes into  their body  and mandates for state employees and  healthcare workers go too far  but in europe italy has gone even  further from october 15 anyone wanting  to participate in public life will have  to be vaccinated or have recovered from  a coveted infection  the penalty will be a hefty fine  [Music]  many are now asking whether these new  rules are part of a trend that will lead  to a global vaccine mandate  it could be the only way to beat the  covert pandemic  hi i'm ben fazoulen the number of  coronavirus cases continues to rise in  greece and now that schools have resumed  following the summer break the  government's introduced tougher  legislation aimed at persuading more  people to get vaccinated  it's lunchtime at this fish restaurant  near athens  everyone who works here is vaccinated  the owner checked their vaccination  certificates  having all eight of his employees  inoculated is the only way his  establishment could qualify his covet  free and operate without restrictions  there was one employee who didn't want  to get vaccinated at first but when it  essentially became mandatory he did it  was the only way to keep his job  at first the new rules only applied to  workers in bars and restaurants  but now everyone has to inform employers  of their status  unvaccinated people have to be tested at  least once a week at their own expense  employers who do not enforce the rules  can be fined up to 1500 euros most  support this policy  it protects us all  i think that it's crucial for the common  good i'm a teacher  how can i enter a class without being  vaccinated you just can't do that  the rules in the health care sector are  especially strict  those who refuse to get vaccinated have  been put on unpaid leave  that has triggered protests and left  many hospitals with a real problem  40 of our 1600 employees have been  suspended  unfortunately that has a real impact  because we have serious staff shortages  and that's true for almost all the  hospitals in the country  at the biggest vaccination center in  athens things are quiet  only around 56 percent of greeks are  fully vaccinated and infection rates  remain high  the government says it might have to get  tougher still to boost the vaccination  rate  we first have to evaluate the data and  then see where mandatory vaccination may  be beneficial for other professional  groups where gentle persuasion hasn't  worked greece is opting for a tougher  line with ever stricter rules and  penalties  arthur kaplan is director of the  division of medical ethics at the nyu  grossman school of medicine so should  the covert vaccine be mandatory in your  opinion  i think it should we've tried to educate  people to choose the vaccination we've  tried to incentivize them in many  countries including the us with rewards  free meals drinks  lotteries that they could win  but we still have a hardcore population  here in the us and around the world  that's not vaccinating and the only way  out  of continuing to live under the shadow  of covid is to boost those vaccination  rates i think mandates will do it  is it ethical to force someone to get it  by law  i think it's very ethical people say  it's my choice it's my body i should be  able to choose but in a pandemic the  principle is don't kill your neighbor  don't harm vulnerable people who have  immune diseases or children who can't  yet be vaccinated that's got to be the  dominant ethical principle that leads  directly to the justification of  mandates  looking at the u.s what's the point of a  mandate for teachers in many cases but  in most cases not for the students when  you're talking about harming your  neighbor  right well in the u.s we don't yet have  approval to vaccinate under 12. so the  teachers want to be protected from  literally the students who might bring  covid into the classroom  the u.s hasn't been aggressive enough in  my view in testing so that we can't  assume that people will home test and  then not send their children to school  so it's very important to protect  teachers janitors school personnel when  the kids can't be vaccinated so that's  the rationale  and what about health care workers a lot  of hospitals are losing much needed  staff because of these new new rules  well look it is a danger that people may  still hold out and say as a hospital  assistant a nurse i'm not going to work  if you make me vaccinate i actually  think the numbers are not bearing that  out we've had mandates in a number of  health systems where i am in connecticut  nearby in new york we've lost a very few  people despite all the noise and  rhetoric they haven't quit they don't  want to become unemployed they don't  want to lose their pension and if we  push a universal mandate through in  health care there's nowhere else to go  to work so i i think that the staff  shortages  maybe could be a small problem but look  you don't want your nurse or your  nurse's aid to harm you or your loved  one if they're in the healthcare setting  i think you got to stick with the  vaccine  of course not but  is it really such a small problem when  we see that so much of this skepticism  is among health professionals  well it's a problem when health  professionals don't follow the science  it's a problem when they're not good  role models  sadly many of them are subjected to  misinformation  sadly some of them are also worried  about issues like fertility which is not  an issue for the vaccines so  you know you can't let fear and  misinformation drive health care  uh policy the other issue is you know  there's burnout in a different way if we  don't vaccinate them and our hospitals  get overwhelmed the nurses the doctors  burn out they get tired that leads to  staff shortages too and if they get  covered that leads to staff shortages so  it's not sort of vaccinate and risk  losing people i think it's more accurate  to say vaccinate and perhaps some people  will quit but you're not going to lose  people because they get sick arthur can  you tell us what the research actually  says about how well vaccine mandates  work  i've done a lot of research myself in a  different vaccine for the flu we push  that through the united states as a  requirement for health care workers  at my own school before we mandated flu  shots we had a vaccination rate about 77  percent in a very big health system  today it's 99.4 percent mandates will  work employees get used to the idea that  they have to vaccinate as a condition of  employment i think the protests will  calm down once we institute the mandates  in health care settings  so is the mandate our only chance to get  out of this pandemic  well look vaccination is key but i think  we've got to do a couple of other things  you do want to test  more testing home testing making sure  you know  some countries are testing once a week  for the unvaccinated that's not enough  testing is a crucial tool and that ought  to be pushed more and hopefully we'll  find some medicines that might act as  preventative many drug companies are  working on those so if we had that one  two kind of combination vaccination plus  preventative medication then i think  that's the road out  arthur kaplan great to have you on the  show today pleasure talking to you  thank you  a brief look at a couple covert stories  making news picking up on our topic  today of vaccine mandates  new york hospitals have begun firing or  suspending health care workers for  defying a state order to get the covert  19 vaccine that's led to staff shortages  forcing some hospitals to delay elective  surgery and reduce services  lobbyists say the measures won't only  hurt patients but also affect hospital  revenues  and japanese prime minister yoshihida  suga says his country is lifting a  coronavirus state of emergency in all  regions  the order's been in place for nearly  half a year daily cases fell from 25 000  last month to just over a thousand on  monday japan plans a gradual reopening  so some restrictions will remain in  place  and lastly let me hand you over to our  science correspondent derek williams  with one of your questions on the  coronavirus  is vaccine immune response all about the  levels of antibodies they induce or are  there other factors to consider  an antibody  is a roughly y-shaped protein produced  by the body that's able to recognize and  dock on to the outer surface of an  invading pathogen like a virus by  docking on  the antibodies might neutralize it  directly by by blocking the mechanisms  that allow it to to invade a cell or  they might flag up the invader for  destruction by other layers of the  immune system's defenses  it takes some time for the immune system  to learn how to tailor  specific antibodies to to specific  pathogens so  the levels of those particular  antibodies are often used as a kind of  rule of thumb  when it comes to judging immune status  if someone has a lot of the antibodies  specific to sars cov2  then that's viewed as a sign that the  person is unlikely to get a severe case  of of covet 19. but it's a lot more  complicated than that because in  particular what are called b cells and t  cells also play  vital roles in protection both during an  infection and in immunity down the road  antibody levels will tend to drop off in  the months after infection or  vaccination experts say because your  body doesn't keep churning out  antibodies to every disease you've ever  been exposed to  at high levels forever that would be  a waste of resources instead  immune system cells store long-term  information on known pathogens  and how to make antibodies against them  if you're exposed then again later they  recognize the invader and they can  quickly ramp back up antibody production  and immune defenses  um in talking about immune response or  or a lack of it however  we tend to focus on antibody levels  because  they're a fairly straightforward metric  to measure  but they're really just one factor in in  a very complex system  [Music]  nice to have you along stay safe and see  you again soon bye-bye  [Music]  you

